  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 347 AM CDT Fri Oct 23 2026\n\nOver the western CONUS, a deep upper-level trough propagates to the \nnortheast toward the central CONUS early next week. Guidance \nindicates that as this trough slowly moves to the northeast, its \namplitude will decrease enough to the point where the cold front \nassociated with the trough will stay well to the north of our \nforecast region Tuesday into Wednesday, and allow for a ridge to \nremain over the region. This will allow for continued dry and \nseasonably warm conditions to continue through this forecast period \nwith high temperatures remaining in the 70s each day. Another trough \nand associated cold front then begin to build over the western CONUS \nbefore moving to the east toward the end of the week. Guidance is \nuncertain on the exact timing and magnitude of this cold front in \nMissouri. NBM guidance from yesterday had IQRs of 15-20 degrees on \nThursday and Friday with the 25th percentiles in the lower to mid-\n60s and 75th percentiles near 80, but the most recent runs have \nbrought the IQRs closer to 10-15 degrees with 25th percentiles \nbrought up into the 70s on Thursday and mid to upper 60s on Friday \nwhile keeping the 75th percentiles near 80. This hints that model \nguidance is either pushing the cold front further into the future or \ndecreasing the magnitude of the front. Latest ensemble guidance has \nincreased the chances of rain associated with moisture ahead of the \nfront with a 20-50 percent chance of measurable rain by Friday \nevening across the region.",
